# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is not.

# Trigger.dev — dashboard → API Keys
# Use the DEV key locally. The PROD key goes in Vercel's environment settings,
# never in a file here.
TRIGGER_SECRET_KEY=
TRIGGER_PROJECT_ID=

# Supabase — dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys
#
# Both of these are NEXT_PUBLIC_, meaning they are compiled into the JavaScript
# the browser downloads. That is intended: the publishable key can't do anything
# on its own. What stops one account reading another's leads is the row-level
# security policies in supabase/schema.sql, not the secrecy of this key.
#
# The same values go in Vercel. They are the only keys in this project that are
# safe in both places.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is deliberately absent, and must stay absent.
#
# It ignores every row-level security policy. Nothing in this project uses it,
# and `npm run check-env` fails if it finds one. If a change appears to need it,
# the design has drifted — fix the design.

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is deliberately absent, and must stay absent.
#
# The task calls Claude; the frontend never does. The key lives in the
# Trigger.dev dashboard — set for both DEV and PROD — and nowhere else. Not in
# Vercel, not in the browser, not in git, not on disk.
#
# Local iteration still works: `npx trigger.dev dev` runs the task on your
# machine and pulls the key from the dashboard's DEV environment. Note that a
# local .env value would override the dashboard's, which is exactly why one
# shouldn't exist here. See CLAUDE.md.
